{{ framework }} cannot run {{ model }} on this machine's GPU as it is: {{ what }} -- {{ reason }}.
Establish the patch that makes it run, correctly, before anything is measured. The framework's
interpreter is {{ python }}.

 - Clone the upstream source of the package that needs the change (the one {{ python }} has
   installed, at its latest default branch) into {{ repos }}/<its name>, unless a clone is
   there; record its HEAD as the base. Work on a branch `xe-forge/<what>` and commit the patch
   there, so HEAD is the base plus the patch.
 - Add it the way the framework adds one: read its docs for new models or ops and copy the
   structure of the closest one it already has. A plain, correct implementation first --
   torch, or the framework's own portable kernels; making it fast is Xe-Forge's job later.
   Nothing that only hides the gap: no generic fallback backend, no CPU path, no emulation of
   a number format this device has no kernel for (that is {"error": ...}, not a patch).
 - Build it into a venv layered over {{ python }}, holding only what you rebuilt, and check
   that the patched package imports from there and the framework still imports.
{% if workload == "text" %} - Write generate.sh here: under $FRAMEWORK_PYTHON, run {{ model }} with the framework, on
   the GPU, eagerly, on `xe_forge.frontend.profile.token_ids(profile.hf_vocab(tok),
   int($BATCH), int($INPUT_LEN), $PROMPT_FILE)`, generating $OUTPUT_LEN tokens with no stop at
   EOS, plain greedy -- the argmax of the raw logits, no repetition, frequency or presence
   penalty, the checkpoint's generation_config ignored -- and write the generated ids to
   $OUT/tokens.json as [[ids of sequence 0], ...]. The check sets those four variables (a
   natural-text prompt, the tokens it scores); read them from the environment, no defaults.
{% else %} - Write generate.sh here: under $FRAMEWORK_PYTHON, run {{ model }} with the framework, on
   the GPU, eagerly, with the prompt {{ prompt | tojson }}{% if negative_prompt %} (negative
   prompt {{ negative_prompt | tojson }}){% endif %} and `seed=42`, one output, and write into $OUT:
     - `prompt_embeds.npy` -- what the text encoder produced. One small tensor, and an op the
       transformer shares with the encoder changes it too, so it cannot be assumed unchanged;
     - `latent_step1.npy` -- the latent after the **first** denoise step, and
       `latent_final.npy` -- the latent after the last, before the VAE. Both float32 `.npy`
       of the array as the model holds it;
     - `output.png`{% if frames > 1 %}, plus one float32 `frames/f0000.npy` per frame, in order. The encoded video
       cannot be compared: the encoder is lossy, so it hides this difference and adds its own{% endif %}.
   Keep it short: `XE_FORGE_CHECK_STEPS` steps, default 1. The sampling loop feeds each step
   into the next, so a rounding difference grows with every step; at step 1 it has not grown
   yet, and a real defect still stands out by orders of magnitude. Switch off anything that
   makes one run differ from the next: CPU or layerwise offload, autotuning.
 - generate.sh must also honour `XE_FORGE_NATIVE=1`: the same run, with the framework's own
   plain-torch path instead of your new op -- vLLM's `custom_ops: ["none"]` (or `-<op name>`
   for one op) and the torch/SDPA attention backend. Nothing else changes: same weights, same
   seed, same precision. That is the reference the check compares you against, and the two
   wall times are the op gain your patch is worth.
{% endif %}
 - Write patch.json here: {"python": "<the layered venv's python>", "repo": "<the clone's
   directory name under {{ repos }}>", "base": "<base commit>", "package": "<the module the
   patch changes, importable>"}, plus "reference_python": "<an interpreter with the GPU torch
   and a transformers that loads {{ model }}>" when {{ python }}'s cannot (a venv over its
