   its own code. Then run
     xe-forge-skill patch check . --repos {{ repos }} --framework-python {{ python }} \
       --model {{ model }} --batch {{ batch }}{% if workload == "text" %} --input-len {{ input_len }}{% if 'prompts' in inputs %} \
       --prompt-file {{ inputs.prompts.file }}{% endif %}{% if precision is defined and precision.startswith(("float", "bfloat")) %} \
       --precision {{ precision }}{% endif %}{% endif %}
   It diffs base..HEAD itself, applies that to a fresh clone, checks the import, runs
   generate.sh, then compares its output against a reference.
{% if workload == "text" %}   The reference is transformers, scoring your tokens on the GPU: fed each sequence's prompt
   and generated prefix, every generated token must be within 0.3 nat of transformers' best. It scores in {{ precision if precision is defined and precision.startswith(("float", "bfloat")) else "bf16" }}; a sequence that fails there is judged again in
   float32 over several slightly perturbed forwards, a token failing only by more than the
   reference's own spread there. The reference runs the checkpoint as it is specified,
   activation quantization included: a dequantized one is stable and wrong.
{% else %}   The reference is generate.sh again with `XE_FORGE_NATIVE=1`, and the two outputs are
   compared stage by stage (`quality.json`): the earliest stage present is the gate, because
   the later ones have amplified the difference. The op gain is reported with the verdict.
{% endif %}   A FAIL there is the patch's: fix it, never the check.

